NetHack is old, and it's directly derived from the Hack
codebase, which is right properly ancient.  Back then, they
didn't keep any data that weren't absolutely necessary, even
if they only took up a few bytes, because every few bytes
mattered.  Computer resources were much scarcer back
then.  Some of the 8-bit micros had access to less than a
hundred kilobytes of memory and less than 200 kilobytes
of secondary storage.  Programmers had to economize.
NetHack doesn't scrimp nearly as much as some things
did (it does not, for example, squeeze four characters of
text into three bytes, thus saving less than 25% on text
string storage, like e.g. Zork), but by today's standards it
is nonetheless extremely miserly.

